#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""magician ste-lint — STE-lite checker for Markdown prose (stdlib only, no network, read-only).

Checks written deliverables (specs, plans, research notes, skill bodies) against the checkable part
of magician's STE-lite ruleset: a paraphrase of a few Simplified Technical English habits, with
magician's own short avoid/use word list. It ships no rule text and no dictionary of the ASD-STE100
standard and claims no compliance with it. The tool never modifies a file.

Usage:
  ste-lint [options] <file.md>...      check one or more Markdown files
  ste-lint [options] --stdin           check Markdown read from stdin (reported as <stdin>)
  ste-lint -h | --help

Options:
  --max N          print at most N findings (default 10; 0 = no cap); the total is always reported
  --json           one JSON object on stdout instead of lines
  --terms FILE     allow-list, one term per line (a word, identifier or sentence-initial word that
                   a rule must not flag); .magician/ste-terms in the current folder loads by itself
  --strict         heuristic (warn) rules count as errors

Output lines:  path:line: RULE: message        (an error, from a deterministic rule)
               path:line: warn RULE: message   (a warning, from a heuristic rule)

Exit codes:  0 no errors (warnings alone still exit 0, except with --strict)
             1 at least one error
             2 usage error, or a file that cannot be read

Excluded from every check: YAML frontmatter, fenced code blocks, inline code, URLs and link
targets, tables, blockquotes and "quoted text", headings and HTML comments. Inline code, a path,
a number with its unit, a hyphenated group and a quoted span count as one word each; a
parenthetical counts as one word of its sentence and is measured on its own.

Deterministic rules (errors): S1 S2 P1 V2 V3 W2 W3 W4 W6 C1.
Heuristic rules (warnings): S3 S4 S5 V1 V4 W1 N1 L1.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import re
import sys

